<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Strasbourg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/357">357</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Argentoratum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Strasbourg, or Argentoratum, was fought in August 357 near Strasbourg between a Roman army under the Caesar Julian and a coalition of Alemannic kings led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chnodomar">Chnodomar</a> and his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapio">Serapio</a>. Julian's roughly 13,000 men defeated a force that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> put at some 35,000; Chnodomar was captured and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The victory restored Roman control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> frontier and made Julian's reputation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>During the 3rd century the small tribes of free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> coalesced into large, loose confederations: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, the Alamanni and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>. The Alamanni colonised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agri-decumates">Agri Decumates</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> evacuated it in the mid-3rd century and formed small cantons, mostly along the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. In 350 the usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnentius">Magnentius</a> killed the emperor Constans, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a> made a truce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> to deal with him. The Franks and Alamanni used the absence of the best Roman forces to overrun much of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a>. They captured many Roman forts on the Rhine and set up permanent camps on the west bank, and more than 20,000 Roman civilians were reported to have been abducted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Julian came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> with a small escort of 360 men and learned on the way that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> had fallen to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. In 356 he joined the main Gaul army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> and decided to march to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dieuze">Dieuze</a> his army was ambushed, and two legions were rescued only when other regiments heard the uproar. A deserter told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chnodomar">Chnodomar</a> that Julian had 13,000 men at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saverne">Saverne</a>, and Libanius implies 15,000. Ammianus puts the Alamanni at around 35,000, a figure several historians regard as unrealistically high; about 25,000 is considered the most likely.</p><p>The army marched 21 Roman miles from probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saverne">Saverne</a>. Ammianus reports that Julian urged a postponement, but his officers and men demanded battle at once. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chnodomar">Chnodomar</a> held the Alamannic left with his cavalry and mixed light infantry among the horsemen to stab the horses of the Roman cataphracts. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapio">Serapio</a>, still a teenager, commanded the right. When the German ranks demanded that Chnodomar and the chiefs dismount and lead the foot, he complied and so lost control of the battle. The Roman cavalry initially performed poorly, but the Roman infantry won the day and drove the Alamanni beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, with negligible Roman losses. About 6,000 Alamanni were counted dead on the field.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After the battle Julian was acclaimed Augustus by his troops but refused the title, which only the ruling Augustus, Constantius, could bestow. All Alamanni families who had settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> were rounded up and expelled from imperial territory. The battle was the turning point in Julian's effort to restore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> frontier. Until then he had campaigned largely inside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, with the barbarian bands holding the initiative. Afterwards he invaded the lands beyond the Rhine each year, devastated them and imposed tributary status on the tribes, and repaired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s line of forts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Strasbourg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Julian restored it, and in 357 at the <b>Battle of Strasbourg</b> his army of about 13,000 defeated a coalition under Chnodomar and his nephew Serapio, which Ammianus Marcellinus put at some 35,000 men.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Military</a></li></ul></section>
